## One token, then the next

GPT-2 only ever does one thing: given some text, it gives a probability for every possible next
token. That's the whole stack from Part IV, run once. Writing a paragraph is just a loop around it:

1. run the model on the text so far,
2. pick a token from its probabilities,
3. add that token to the end of the text,
4. repeat.

Every model in a chat app writes this way. Try it yourself: each press runs the full model once.
Click any word in the list to pick it instead of the model's favorite.

<GlassBoxGenerator
	title="Glass-box generator"
	presets={[{ trace: 'the-cat-sat', label: 'The cat sat on the' }]}
	branches={['the-cat-sat-bed', 'the-cat-sat-couch', 'the-cat-sat-ground', 'the-cat-sat-edge']}
/>

Keep pressing *Add the most likely* and GPT-2 writes "The cat sat on the floor, and the cat was
still asleep." Go back and pick " bed" instead, and it gets stuck: ", and the cat sat on the bed, and
the cat sat on the bed…". Always taking the single most likely token often falls into loops like
this. Picking at random, weighted by the probabilities (the <GlossaryPopover term="temperature">temperature</GlossaryPopover> spinner from Ch 2), mostly
avoids them, which is one reason most text generators sample instead.<Cite id="holtzman2020" />

## No going back

Each token is chosen once, and everything after it is built on top. The model doesn't plan a
sentence, write a draft, or go back and fix an earlier word. If it picks " bed", it has to make the
best of " bed". And each new token costs a full run through all 124 million of GPT-2's weights, so
a 500-token answer means 500 runs.

## Knowing when to stop

The loop has to end somehow. GPT-2's vocabulary includes a special token, `<|endoftext|>` (number
50,256), that means "end of text". When the model picks it, the program stops.<Cite id="gpt2-config" />
Otherwise, the program stops at a length limit it was given. Chat models work the same way
with their own end-of-turn token.

## Run it live

Everything above is recorded. To type your own beginning, pick any word, and change the temperature,
press *Run it live* in the generator. It downloads a compressed copy of GPT-2 small (about 155 MB
including the code that runs it) and runs it on your device. Nothing you type leaves your browser.

<RecapCard>

- A model only predicts the next token. Generating text is a loop: predict, pick, append, repeat.
- Each token costs one full run of the model, and once picked it is never revised.
- Always taking the top token can loop; sampling with a temperature mostly avoids that.
- Generation stops at a special end token or a length limit.

</RecapCard>

